# Snapshot data is content-addressed. The manifest records a SHA-256 over the
# bytes on disk -- src/data/flight_network.py writes them with write_bytes,
# and Snapshot.verify re-hashes the files it finds -- so any newline
# conversion on checkout breaks every checksum and the snapshot refuses to
# open.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which does exactly that:
# cloning with it set rewrites airports.csv as CRLF, and Snapshot.open then
# raises SnapshotIntegrityError before the data is ever read. Pin these to LF
# whatever the local git config says.
#
# The same rule covers every data file, not only snapshots. Four of them were
# committed with CRLF -- international_airports.csv, routes.dat,
# airport-comments.csv, us-airports.csv -- and are normalised here, so that
# nothing in data/ depends on the platform it was written on.
*.csv text eol=lf
*.dat text eol=lf
